.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt OSSL_PARAM_ALLOCATE_FROM_TEXT 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm OSSL_PARAM_allocate_from_text
.Nd OSSL_PARAM construction utilities
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/params.h
.Ft int
.Fo OSSL_PARAM_allocate_from_text
.Fa "OSSL_PARAM *to"
.Fa "const OSSL_PARAM *paramdefs"
.Fa "const char *key"
.Fa "const char *value"
.Fa "size_t value_n"
.Fa "int *found"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
With OpenSSL before version 3.0, parameters were passed down to or
retrieved from algorithm implementations via control functions.
Some of these control functions existed in variants that took string
parameters, for example
.Xr EVP_PKEY_CTX_ctrl_str 3 .
.Pp
OpenSSL 3.0 introduces a new mechanism to do the same thing with an
array of parameters that contain name, value, value type and value size
(see
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
for more information).
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_allocate_from_text
uses
.Fa key
to look up an item in
.Fa paramdefs .
If an item was found, it converts
.Fa value
to something suitable for that item's
.Em data_type ,
and stores the result in
.Em to->data
as well as its size in
.Em to->data_size .
.Em to->key
and
.Em to->data_type
are assigned the corresponding values from the item that was found, and
.Em to->return_size
is set to zero.
.Pp
.Em to->data
is always allocated using
.Xr OPENSSL_zalloc 3
and needs to be freed by the caller when it's not useful any more, using
.Xr OPENSSL_free 3 .
.Pp
If
.Fa found
is not NULL,
.Em *found
is set to 1 if
.Fa key
could be located in
.Fa paramdefs ,
and to 0 otherwise.
.Ss The use ofkey No andvalue No in detail
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_allocate_from_text
takes note if
.Fa key
starts with "hex", and will only use the rest of
.Fa key
to look up an item in
.Fa paramdefs
in that case.
As an example, if
.Fa key
is "hexid", "id" will be looked up in
.Fa paramdefs .
.Pp
When an item in
.Fa paramdefs
has been found,
.Fa value
is converted depending on that item's
.Em data_type ,
as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy OSSL_PARAM_INTEGER No and Sy OSSL_PARAM_UNSIGNED_INTEGER
If
.Fa key
didn't start with "hex",
.Fa value
is assumed to contain
.Fa value_n
decimal characters, which are decoded, and the resulting bytes become
the number stored in the
.Em to->data
storage.
.Pp
If
.Fa value
starts with "0x", it is assumed to contain
.Fa value_n
hexadecimal characters.
.Pp
If
.Fa key
started with "hex",
.Fa value
is assumed to contain
.Fa value_n
hexadecimal characters without the "0x" prefix.
.Pp
If
.Fa value
contains characters that couldn't be decoded as hexadecimal or decimal
characters,
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_allocate_from_text
considers that an error.
.It Sy OSSL_PARAM_UTF8_STRING
If
.Fa key
started with "hex",
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_allocate_from_text
considers that an error.
.Pp
Otherwise,
.Fa value
is considered a C string and is copied to the
.Em to->data
storage.
On systems where the native character encoding is EBCDIC, the bytes in
.Em to->data
are converted to ASCII.
.It Sy OSSL_PARAM_OCTET_STRING
If
.Fa key
started with "hex",
.Fa value
is assumed to contain
.Fa value_n
hexadecimal characters, which are decoded, and the resulting bytes are
stored in the
.Em to->data
storage.
If
.Fa value
contains characters that couldn't be decoded as hexadecimal or decimal
characters,
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_allocate_from_text
considers that an error.
.Pp
If
.Fa key
didn't start with "hex",
.Fa value_n
bytes from
.Fa value
are copied to the
.Em to->data
storage.
.El
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn OSSL_PARAM_allocate_from_text
returns 1 if
.Fa key
was found in
.Fa paramdefs
and there was no other failure, otherwise 0.
.Sh NOTES
The parameter descriptor array comes from functions dedicated to return
them.
The following
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
attributes are used:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fa key
.It Em data_type
.It Em data_size
.El
All other attributes are ignored.
.Pp
The
.Em data_size
attribute can be zero, meaning that the parameter it describes expects
arbitrary length data.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Code that looked like this:
.Bd -literal
  int mac_ctrl_string(EVP_PKEY_CTX *ctx, const char *value)
  {
      int rv;
      char *stmp, *vtmp = NULL;

      stmp = OPENSSL_strdup(value);
      if (stmp == NULL)
          return -1;
      vtmp = strchr(stmp, ':');
      if (vtmp != NULL)
          *vtmp++ = '\e0';
      rv = EVP_MAC_ctrl_str(ctx, stmp, vtmp);
      OPENSSL_free(stmp);
      return rv;
  }

  ...

  for (i = 0; i < sk_OPENSSL_STRING_num(macopts); i++) {
      char *macopt = sk_OPENSSL_STRING_value(macopts, i);

      if (pkey_ctrl_string(mac_ctx, macopt) <= 0) {
          BIO_printf(bio_err,
                     "MAC parameter error \e"%s\e"\en", macopt);
          ERR_print_errors(bio_err);
          goto mac_end;
      }
  }
.Ed
.Pp
Can be written like this instead:
.Bd -literal
  OSSL_PARAM *params =
      OPENSSL_zalloc(sizeof(*params)
                     * (sk_OPENSSL_STRING_num(opts) + 1));
  const OSSL_PARAM *paramdefs = EVP_MAC_settable_ctx_params(mac);
  size_t params_n;
  char *opt = "<unknown>";

  for (params_n = 0; params_n < (size_t)sk_OPENSSL_STRING_num(opts);
       params_n++) {
      char *stmp, *vtmp = NULL;

      opt = sk_OPENSSL_STRING_value(opts, (int)params_n);
      if ((stmp = OPENSSL_strdup(opt)) == NULL
              || (vtmp = strchr(stmp, ':')) == NULL)
          goto err;

      *vtmp++ = '\e0';
      if (!OSSL_PARAM_allocate_from_text(&params[params_n],
                                         paramdefs, stmp,
                                         vtmp, strlen(vtmp), NULL))
          goto err;
  }
  params[params_n] = OSSL_PARAM_construct_end();
  if (!EVP_MAC_CTX_set_params(ctx, params))
      goto err;
  while (params_n-- > 0)
      OPENSSL_free(params[params_n].data);
  OPENSSL_free(params);
  /* ... */
  return;

 err:
  BIO_printf(bio_err, "MAC parameter error '%s'\en", opt);
  ERR_print_errors(bio_err);
.Ed
